-```markdown
-# Glass Notifications
-
-Puts notifications from a carried Android phone onto Google Glass Explorer
-Edition over classic Bluetooth.
-
-- **`wire`** — pure JVM. Protocol, encoding, framing. All the fiddly logic,
-  tested on the host.
-- **`glass`** — Glass Explorer (AOSP 5.1.1, API 22). RFCOMM server, interrupt
-  overlay, read-only queue.
-- **`phone`** — LG V30 (Android 9, API 28). Notification listener, filtering
-  and tiering, RFCOMM client.
-
-Design: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-04-glass-notifications-design.md`
-
-## Build
-
-    ./gradlew :glass:assembleDebug :phone:assembleDebug
-
-## Install
-
-    adb -s 0123456789ABCDEF install -r glass/build/outputs/apk/debug/glass-debug.apk
-    adb -s VS9967edd915b     install -r phone/build/outputs/apk/debug/phone-debug.apk
-
-## Develop the Glass UI without the phone
-
-    scripts/fake-notify.sh "Signal" "Jordan Reyes" "are you still good for 7pm?" INTERRUPT
-    scripts/fake-notify.sh --clear
-
-## Recovery
-
-Glass refuses connections from any device but the first one that connects.
-Clearing that pin (after a reflash, or when moving to a different phone):
-
-    adb -s 0123456789ABCDEF shell pm clear dev.erinlkolp.glassnotify.glass
-
-## Testing note
-
-`adb shell input` injects below the window manager and cannot reproduce
-touchable-region or multitouch behaviour. Anything touch-related must be
-tested with a real finger on the real device.
